The matching goes back to the way it was on 2026-11-19

The photograph of run 543, taken on 2026-11-19 at 15:20, is what this board
looked like when the matching worked:

  nio: start> com.apple.driver.AppleARFPlatform
  nio: kext start ret=0x0 id=[com.apple.driver.AppleARFPlatform]
  P1
  nio: P ArmCpu r=0
  P:nic: ufs probe #25 provider=cpu7 compatible="qcom,kryo"
  nio: P NioUfs r=0
  P1
  nio: P AppleARMCPU r=0xfffffffff16ff2d400

The platform expert started, and three drivers were probed.  Everything
since then has been worse: the matching stopped, and the round after this
one spent its time finding out why.  Three changes after that photograph
are undone here.

The matching goes back to the boot thread.  On its own thread it ran at
the same time as the rest of the boot and met somebody who was holding a
registry lock: the waypoint said 40, which is the name comparison, and the
thread never came back.  On the boot thread it is the board's own order:
publish the personalities, match, carry on.

The personality matches on the name again.  The name was empty in the
rounds that followed -- the tree is published when the platform expert
starts, and the platform expert did not start because the matching was
stuck -- but in the rounds that worked, the name was there and the
personality asked for it.

And the publication goes back inside StartIOKitMatching(), where it was
before it was moved earlier: the name the personality asks for is the
nub's name at the moment of the publication, and the later it is, the
more certainly the tree has been read.

The probes stay: they are what found this.

